          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do European and US bodies differ on when to start medication for stage 1 hypertension?",
          "answer": "**Yes, they differ, mainly because they define the blood pressure ranges differently.**\n\nThe US position uses 130/80 mm Hg as the diagnostic threshold. European guidance starts hypertension at 140/90 mm Hg and treats readings of 130 to 139 / 80 to 89 as high-normal rather than stage 1 disease (Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets). Because of this, much of the population that US guidance calls stage 1 is advised to try lifestyle changes alone under European guidance (Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line).\n\nThe guidance also sets out a tiered approach for stage 1 hypertension. Drug treatment alongside lifestyle measures is suggested when there is established cardiovascular disease, diabetes, chronic kidney disease, or an estimated 10-year cardiovascular risk of 10 percent or more. Without those features, lifestyle measures come first, with reassessment after three to six months before medication is considered (Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line). The passages do not say which body this tiered rule comes from, so I can't attribute it to the US or Europe.\n\nThe retrieved material does not give the full European drug-start criteria for stage 1, so I can't set out a side-by-side comparison of the two rules. A clinician can explain how these guidelines apply to a specific person's readings and risk.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
